```java
static void modify(int[] arr) {
    arr[0] = 999; // same object the caller points to
}
int[] nums = {10, 20};
modify(nums);
System.out.println(nums[0]); // 999 — element change is visible
```
To actually replace the array for the caller, return the new array and let the caller reassign it.

```java
public class ArrayPassingDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] numbers = {10, 20, 30};
        modifyElements(numbers);
        System.out.println(numbers[0]); // 999 — element change visible

        reassignArray(numbers);
        System.out.println(numbers[0]); // 999 — reassign had NO effect

        numbers = createNewArray();     // caller reassigns to replace
        System.out.println(numbers[0]); // 100
    }
    static void modifyElements(int[] arr) { arr[0] = 999; }
    static void reassignArray(int[] arr) { arr = new int[]{1, 2, 3}; }
    static int[] createNewArray() { return new int[]{100, 200, 300}; }
}
```
